<body>
<nav class="guide-nav">
  <a href="/research/">← Research</a>
  <span class="logo">Seascape Vacations</span>
</nav>

<main class="page">
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="hero-copy">
      <span class="eyebrow">Owner Benchmark</span>
      <span class="scope-pill">5-property Gulf Coast scope</span>
      <h1>Your fee is not the only leak.</h1>
      <p class="hero-lead">Most owners compare management percentages and stop there. The bigger owner-net question is the full stack: management fee, OTA drag, direct-booking cost, rate discipline, and the operating misses that quietly weaken future payout.</p>
      <p class="hero-support">This benchmark is built for owners already wondering whether the current setup is busy but under-earning. It does not promise a market-wide answer. It shows the fee math Seascape can defend today, then labels the scenario math separately.</p>
      <div class="proof-rail">
        {% for label in proofLabels %}
        <span class="proof-chip">{{ label }}</span>
        {% endfor %}
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="hero-side">
      <div class="side-panel">
        <span class="eyebrow">Observed Seascape portfolio data</span>
        <div class="stats-grid">
          {% for stat in benchmark.stats %}
          <div class="stat-card">
            <strong>{{ stat.value }}</strong>
            <span>{{ stat.label }}</span>
            <p>{{ stat.detail }}</p>
          </div>
          {% endfor %}
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="side-panel">
        <span class="eyebrow">How To Read This</span>
        <ul class="quick-list">
          <li>Observed Seascape numbers stay inside the current five-home operating set.</li>
          <li>Property examples show where the leak is already visible.</li>
          <li>Scenario math is there to help owners audit the current setup, not to promise a future result.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="source-note">
    <p><strong>Source note:</strong> {{ benchmark.sourceLabel }}. {{ benchmark.methodology }} This page keeps the practical owner hook above the fold and moves proof language down here where it belongs.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="section-intro">
      <span class="eyebrow">Leak Stack</span>
      <h2>Why the headline fee is the wrong comparison</h2>
      <p>Owners usually see the management percentage first, even though that is only one layer of owner drag. The faster audit is to ask where the payout is leaking before and after the management fee line ever gets discussed.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="stack-grid">
      <article class="stack-card">
        <span class="stack-metric">Headline fee</span>
        <h3>Management fee</h3>
        <p>The fee still matters, but it is only one piece of the owner-net equation. A lower-looking manager can still underperform if the calendar, channel mix, or operations are weak.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="stack-card">
        <span class="stack-metric">Observed Seascape portfolio data</span>
        <h3>OTA host drag</h3>
        <p>Airbnb's fee is not just a platform line item. The observed Seascape benchmark shows 13.4% host-fee drag on post-October 2025 bookings, including fees applied to the full subtotal.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="stack-card">
        <span class="stack-metric">Observed Seascape portfolio data</span>
        <h3>Direct payment cost</h3>
        <p>Direct processing at about 2.9% does not solve everything by itself. It does show how much owner margin can move when a booking is acquired without the heavier OTA layer.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="stack-card">
        <span class="stack-metric">Property-level review</span>
        <h3>Rate and operations discipline</h3>
        <p>Discount-heavy fills, weak premium-week protection, turnover misses, and poor owner communication rarely show up cleanly in a flat management-fee comparison. They still hit the same payout.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="section-intro">
      <span class="eyebrow">Channel Gap</span>
      <h2>The clearest math is usually channel math</h2>
      <p>The benchmark is strongest when it shows where the owner-net gap appears first. That is often the difference between expensive OTA drag and lower-cost direct payment processing, before an owner ever argues over one more point of management fee.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-grid">
      <div class="compare-panel">
        <span class="eyebrow">Observed Seascape portfolio data</span>
        <h3>Channel-cost comparison</h3>
        <div class="bar-list">
          <div class="bar-row">
            <div class="bar-label">Airbnb host drag</div>
            <div class="bar-track"><span class="bar-fill" style="width:89%;background:var(--gold)"></span></div>
            <div class="bar-value">13.4%</div>
          </div>
          <div class="bar-row">
            <div class="bar-label">Direct payment cost</div>
            <div class="bar-track"><span class="bar-fill" style="width:19%;background:var(--brand-dark)"></span></div>
            <div class="bar-value">2.9%</div>
          </div>
          <div class="bar-row">
            <div class="bar-label">Gap to audit</div>
            <div class="bar-track"><span class="bar-fill" style="width:70%;background:var(--brand)"></span></div>
            <div class="bar-value">10.5 pts</div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="compare-panel">
        <span class="eyebrow">What Owners Usually Miss</span>
        <ul class="quick-list">
          <li>Airbnb takes its cut on the full subtotal, including cleaning-heavy stays.</li>
          <li>A booking can look fine on occupancy while still leaking margin through the channel mix.</li>
          <li>The owner question is not whether direct fixes everything. It is whether the current mix is more expensive than it needs to be.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="section-intro">
      <span class="eyebrow">Observed Property Example</span>
      <h2>Where the benchmark already shows real leak patterns</h2>
      <p>These are examples, not universal promises. They are here to keep the page grounded in current Seascape proof instead of drifting into generic owner content.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="example-grid">
      <article class="example-card">
        <span class="eyebrow">Observed property example</span>
        <h3>Sarasota Luxe</h3>
        <span class="example-metric">32.8%</span>
